# BuildKit reads this file — named after the Dockerfile beside it — instead of
# the context root's .dockerignore. The context is the whole repository (this
# is an npm workspace: `npm ci` needs the root lockfile plus every workspace's
# package.json to resolve consistently), so without this file the root
# `node_modules/` (692M) and every workspace's build output would be uploaded
# to the daemon to build one image.
#
# Everything is excluded, then the paths the build actually reads are added
# back, minus their own node_modules/build output (the image builds its own).
# `apps/web/src/server/ingest-health.ts` reaches across the workspace
# boundary for the one pricing table both languages share (Go's own copy is
# services/ingest/pricing/pricing.go) — that single file has to be in context
# too, or `next build` fails to resolve it regardless of OBSTACK_DATA_MODE.
# Measured context after this file: ~5.8MB (`apps/web` minus `.next` is
# ~5.2MB; `packages/obstack-js` minus `node_modules`/`dist` is ~150KB; the two
# root manifests and the pricing table are ~335KB combined).
*
!package.json
!package-lock.json
!apps/web
!packages/obstack-js
!services/ingest/pricing/prices.json
**/node_modules
apps/web/.next
packages/obstack-js/dist
# `!apps/web` re-admits untracked local files too, and `next build` reads
# `.env.local` on a production build (`@next/env`'s list is
# `.env.production.local`, `.env.local`, `.env.production`, `.env`) — so
# without this line a maintainer's own secrets file joins the build and lands
# in the `build` stage's layer. This image is configured at RUNTIME (D266);
# nothing about it may come from whoever's shell ran the build. The other two
# are local cruft whose only effect is invalidating the `COPY apps/web` layer.
**/.env*
**/.DS_Store
**/*.tsbuildinfo
